window.CST_LESSONS = {"source": "docs/08-training.md", "lessons": [{"n": 1, "title": "Why paper first, and why the record is the product", "html": "<p>A trading idea is worthless until it has a record: what it did, when, at what price, with what risk, and nothing edited afterwards. The system's first job is not to make money; it is to produce a record that would survive a skeptic. That is why every event is written to an append-only ledger with a hash chain (each entry fingerprints the one before it), why the daily anchor is committed to a repository with independent timestamps, and why paper results are labelled paper everywhere they appear.</p><p>Real money comes later, small, and only after the record says the rules work. Not because paper is safe (it is) but because a real-money record without a paper record behind it is an anecdote.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the line \"chain ok\" in every review. If it ever says BROKEN, everything stops.</p>", "look_for": "the line \"chain ok\" in every review. If it ever says BROKEN, everything stops."}, {"n": 2, "title": "Lanes: day, swing, long-term", "html": "<p>The system separates trades by holding period, because each period has different risk, different data needs, and different review cadence.</p><table><tr><th>Lane</th><th>Holds for</th><th>Data</th><th>Risk per trade</th><th>Review</th></tr><tr><td>Day</td><td>minutes to hours</td><td>5-minute bars, same session only</td><td>0.25% of equity</td><td>daily</td></tr><tr><td>Swing</td><td>days to weeks</td><td>daily bars</td><td>0.75% of equity</td><td>weekly</td></tr><tr><td>Long-term</td><td>months</td><td>daily bars, 200-day trend</td><td>2% of equity</td><td>monthly</td></tr></table><p>Each lane has its own position cap, daily-loss circuit breaker, and default mode. Mixing them in one bucket hides which one is working.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the lane tag on every alert, e.g. <code>[swing_pullback]</code>.</p>", "look_for": "the lane tag on every alert, e.g. `[swing_pullback]`."}, {"n": 3, "title": "Signal, intent, order, fill", "html": "<p>Four different things that beginners collapse into \"a trade\":</p><p>1. <b>Signal</b>: a strategy says \"this looks like a setup, here is why.\" No size, no order. Long-term lane stops here. 2. <b>Intent</b>: the signal sized to the lane's risk rules: quantity, limit, stop, target, estimated max loss. The risk gate approves or rejects intents, never signals. 3. <b>Order</b>: the intent sent to the broker, with a broker order id. 4. <b>Fill</b>: the broker reports it executed, at a price, at a time.</p><p>The ledger stores all four separately. A review counts fills, not signals; a signal that was never filled is not a trade.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: in a review, \"Trades opened\" (fills) versus the scan heartbeat's \"signals\". The gap between them is the system being selective.</p>", "look_for": "in a review, \"Trades opened\" (fills) versus the scan heartbeat's \"signals\". The gap between them is the system being selective."}, {"n": 4, "title": "Position sizing from risk, not from conviction", "html": "<p>Every intent is sized so that if the stop is hit, the loss is a fixed fraction of equity (the lane's <code>max_loss_per_trade_pct</code>). A tight stop means more shares; a wide stop means fewer. Conviction does not enter the formula, and that is the point: the same rule, every time, is what makes the record comparable across trades.</p><p>Quantity = (equity x risk fraction) / (entry price - stop price), capped by the lane's maximum position size.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: \"max loss $\" on every approval alert. It should be roughly the same number on every swing proposal (0.75% of equity), whatever the symbol.</p>", "look_for": "\"max loss $\" on every approval alert. It should be roughly the same number on every swing proposal (0.75% of equity), whatever the symbol."}, {"n": 5, "title": "R multiples: the only P&L unit that compares trades", "html": "<p>A trade that risked $400 and made $800 is a 2R win. One that risked $400 and lost $200 is a -0.5R loss. Dollars depend on account size and stop width; R does not. The weekly review reports average R per lane and per strategy, and that is the number to watch over months. A strategy with 40% winners and average winner 2R is profitable; one with 70% winners and average winner 0.3R may not be.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: \"avg R\" in the review tables. Expect noise for the first 20 to 30 closed trades.</p>", "look_for": "\"avg R\" in the review tables. Expect noise for the first 20 to 30 closed trades."}, {"n": 6, "title": "Brackets: the stop and target travel with the order", "html": "<p>A stock entry is sent as a bracket: the entry, a stop-loss order, and a take-profit order, linked so that when one exit fills the other cancels. The exits live at the broker, not on your laptop, so they work even if the system is down. When an exit fills, the runner picks it up at the next tick and attaches it to the entry, so the review sees one trade with one P&amp;L.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: two \"Filled\" alerts for the same symbol days apart: the entry, then the exit.</p>", "look_for": "two \"Filled\" alerts for the same symbol days apart: the entry, then the exit."}, {"n": 7, "title": "A bull call spread, and why the system uses it", "html": "<p>Buying a call is a bet on direction with unlimited upside and a total loss if wrong. A bull call spread buys one call and sells a higher-strike call in the same expiry. It gives up upside beyond the short strike in exchange for a much lower cost and a fixed maximum loss (the debit paid). Max profit is the width between strikes minus the debit.</p><p>Example from a live paper trade on 2026-09-24: SPY 766/771 calls expiring Oct 30, debit 3.02. Width 5. Max profit 1.98 per spread ($198), max loss 3.02 ($302). The system picks the expiry closest to 38 days out (between 30 and 45), the long strike at the money, the short strike 5 higher.</p><p>Defined risk is why options are allowed in the swing lane at all. Undefined-risk positions (naked short options) are not in the system and will not be.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the two contract codes on an approval alert, e.g. <code>SPY261030C00766000 / SPY261030C00771000</code>: symbol, date (26-10-30), C for call, strike x1000.</p>", "look_for": "the two contract codes on an approval alert, e.g. `SPY261030C00766000 / SPY261030C00771000`: symbol, date (26-10-30), C for call, strike x1000."}, {"n": 8, "title": "Reading an option symbol", "html": "<p><code>SPY261030C00768000</code> decodes as: SPY, expiring 2026-10-30, Call, strike 768.000. Every contract in the world has one of these (the OCC symbol). The system refuses to place any option order whose symbol does not parse.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the expiry date in the symbol versus today's date. That is the DTE (days to expiry) the exit rules count down.</p>", "look_for": "the expiry date in the symbol versus today's date. That is the DTE (days to expiry) the exit rules count down."}, {"n": 9, "title": "Mechanical exits for spreads", "html": "<p>Spreads are closed by rules, never by mood:</p><ul><li><b>Profit target</b>: when the spread is worth the debit plus 50% of max profit. The second half of the profit takes longer and carries more risk than the first.</li><li><b>Time</b>: at 21 days to expiry, whatever the P&amp;L. Near expiry, small moves in the underlying cause large swings in the spread's value (gamma), and the edge is gone.</li><li><b>Loss</b>: when the spread has lost half its value. The defined risk already limits the damage; this rule stops capital sitting in a dead position.</li></ul><p>The close is one order at a net credit; the broker expresses a credit as a negative limit price, which the system handles. If the close does not fill, it is re-sent a little lower every 30 minutes.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the reason line on a \"Filled: SELL\" options alert. It names the rule.</p>", "look_for": "the reason line on a \"Filled: SELL\" options alert. It names the rule."}, {"n": 10, "title": "The natural price versus the mid", "html": "<p>Every option has a bid (what buyers will pay) and an ask (what sellers want). The mid is halfway. Orders at the mid are polite and often rest unfilled; orders at the natural price (for a spread you are buying: long leg's ask minus short leg's bid) are marketable and fill. The system pays natural on entries and gives a little on exits, because a position you intended but did not get is not a trade, and a position you cannot get out of is a problem.</p><p>The free quote feed the system uses lags the live market. With real money and options, the $99/month real-time feed is a cost of doing business.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: \"paying natural\" in the reasoning of an options proposal, with the mid shown for comparison.</p>", "look_for": "\"paying natural\" in the reasoning of an options proposal, with the mid shown for comparison."}, {"n": 11, "title": "Approval mode: what your tap means", "html": "<p>Option spreads and day trades are approval mode: the strategy recommends, the gate evaluates, your phone asks, and nothing is submitted until you tap Approve. Swing stock pullbacks are the one lane that places paper orders on its own, so the record can later compare the system's unassisted picks with the trades you approved. Exits never wait for a tap, because a stop-loss that needs one is not protection.</p><p>When you tap Approve you are saying \"the rules produced this, the gate passed it, and I am willing to own the outcome.\" You are not choosing the size, the stop, or the contracts; those are fixed by rules so that your decisions are comparable too. Decline is equally valid and equally recorded. Over time the review will show whether your approvals beat the system's own auto-paper lane. If they do not, that is useful information, not failure.</p><p>Approvals expire because a proposal made at yesterday's close is not the same trade at today's open.</p><p>You get two confirmations for every tap: \"Received\" within seconds (the queue has it) and \"Filled\" or \"Drill complete\" when the runner acts (it did it). Two confirmations because they answer two different questions, and a system that only answers one of them leaves you guessing about the other.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: in the review's Discipline section, approvals granted, declined, and expired. Expired should be zero.</p>", "look_for": "in the review's Discipline section, approvals granted, declined, and expired. Expired should be zero."}, {"n": 12, "title": "Reading the weekly review", "html": "<p>Order of attention:</p><p>1. <b>Chain ok.</b> If not, stop. 2. <b>Discipline</b>: gate rejections, expired approvals. These tell you whether the process ran as designed. 3. <b>Trades opened and closed</b>: is the system active at roughly the rate you expect? 4. <b>Avg R and expectancy by strategy</b>: the edge, if any. Meaningless below about 20 closed trades; directional at 50; trustworthy at 100 plus. 5. <b>Max drawdown</b>: the worst peak-to-trough fall in equity. This is the number that decides how much real money you could stand to run. 6. <b>Realized P&amp;L in dollars</b>: last, because it is the most affected by luck and account size.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the temptation to change a rule after a bad week. Write it down in your Friday note instead.</p><p>A note on infrastructure honesty: on the very first day, the cloud runner ran the scan three hours late and sent no alerts because one setting was missing. It was found the same night, fixed, and written into the incident log. That log is part of the record too; a system that hides its failures cannot be trusted with its successes.</p>", "look_for": "the temptation to change a rule after a bad week. Write it down in your Friday note instead."}, {"n": 13, "title": "What \"live\" would take", "html": "<p>Not a switch. A sequence: 10 paper sessions of the swing lane with P&amp;L reconciled to the broker (Checkpoint 1 exit), phone approvals proven (Checkpoint 2 exit), a fresh account and frozen rules for the evidence period (Checkpoint 4), then months of that record before a small real account (Checkpoint 5) with tiny size and approval mode on everything. Each gate has a written exit criterion in <code>docs/06-status-and-checkpoints.md</code>. Nothing in the software can skip a gate; the live interlock requires two independent settings that are absent.</p><p>Regulatory notes to know now: the pattern day trader rule requires $25,000 in a margin account for frequent intraday trading; performance claims made to others are regulated and need a securities attorney before any public product.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the checkpoint status doc. It is the map.</p>", "look_for": "the checkpoint status doc. It is the map."}, {"n": 14, "title": "Reading the Command Center", "html": "<p>The Command Center is a web page, not a control panel. It exists so that you can see the whole paper system in one glance without opening the Alpaca app or scrolling through alerts. Read it top to bottom, and stop as soon as you have what you came for.</p><p><b>Start with Today.</b> The first sentence is the whole point of the page. It is built from four facts, in order of importance: is the ledger chain ok (if not, everything else is irrelevant); is a proposal waiting for you; is the market open; when is the next run. Everything under that line is detail you may or may not want.</p><p><b>\"N/3 conditions\" is setup progress, not a prediction.</b> In the video that inspired this page, each coin carried a \"confidence 100\" number. The Command Center deliberately does not do that. The three bars under each symbol are the three rules of the swing-pullback strategy, in the order the code checks them: the 20-day average is above the 50-day (uptrend); price is within one ATR of the 20-day (a pullback, not a breakdown); today closed up (the pullback is turning). Three of three means a setup exists under the rules. It says nothing about whether the trade will work; the historical edge of a rule is a separate question that only the record can answer.</p><p><b>The three pills.</b> LT is price against its 200-day average (the long-term lane's own rule). SWING is the 20-day against the 50-day. TODAY is the day's change. They are facts, colored so you can see them from across the room, and they can disagree with each other; that is normal and is exactly why the lanes are separate.</p><p><b>The verdict is from the last scan, not from now.</b> \"Needs you\", \"Plan placed\", \"Idea (alert-only)\", \"Gate refused\", \"No setup\" describe what the engine decided at 3:17 PM. Prices under them may be newer. If a verdict and a price seem to conflict, the verdict is still the last thing the system did.</p><p><b>Why there are no buttons.</b> A page that shows a proposal and lets you approve it next to a live price is a page that invites a decision made on the price, not on the plan. Decisions live on the phone alert, which shows the plan (side, size, stop, max loss, expiry) and nothing else. The page tells you a decision is waiting and sends you back to the phone.</p><p><b>The live log is the ledger in plain English.</b> Every row is a hash-chained event, newest first, rewritten as a sentence. Red rows are gate refusals and failures; yellow rows are approvals waiting; green rows are fills and placements. If a row looks wrong, it is the ledger that is right; the sentence template is what needs fixing, and you tell me.</p><p><b>\"chain ok\" in the Record tile</b> is the same integrity check the Friday review runs. If it ever reads BROKEN, the page will say so in the Today line and you stop.</p><p class=\"lookfor\">Look for: the moment the Today line changes from \"One proposal is waiting\" to \"Nothing needs you\" after you tap on the phone. The page did not do that; your tap did, and the next tick recorded it.</p>", "look_for": "the moment the Today line changes from \"One proposal is waiting\" to \"Nothing needs you\" after you tap on the phone. The page did not do that; your tap did, and the next tick recorded it."}], "glossary": {"Anchor": "the day's ledger hash committed to the repository as an independent timestamp.", "Bracket": "entry plus linked stop and target orders.", "Command Center": "the read-only web page that shows the paper system in plain English; it cannot place, approve or cancel anything.", "DTE": "days to expiry.", "Debit / credit": "money paid to open / received to close a spread, per share; multiply by 100 per contract.", "Expectancy": "average P&L per closed trade.", "Intent": "a sized, risk-checked order proposal before it reaches the broker.", "Ledger": "the append-only, hash-chained record (Supabase Postgres in the cloud, SQLite locally).", "Mid / natural": "halfway between bid and ask / the price that fills immediately.", "R": "P&L divided by the risk taken at entry.", "Reconcile": "the runner asking the broker what filled and recording it.", "Route tick": "the hourly job that applies decisions, places approved orders, manages exits, reconciles.", "Tick": "one scheduled run of the system."}};
